WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.005
- [Narrator] This is actress Brie Larson,

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:08.174
and, yes, she's pushing a Jeep.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.468
But when she started her
demanding physical training

00:00:10.468 --> 00:00:13.763
for "Captain Marvel," she
couldn't even do a proper push-up,

00:00:13.763 --> 00:00:15.515
according to her trainer.

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:17.600
Larson joins the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.603
as Carol Danvers in "Captain Marvel."

00:00:20.603 --> 00:00:23.064
To prepare for this career-defining role,

00:00:23.064 --> 00:00:25.233
Larson had to get in shape.

00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:27.027
She trained with Jason Walsh —

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:30.697
he runs Rise Movement and
Rise Nation in Los Angeles —

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.198
the man who's helped actors like

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:36.786
Matt Damon, John Krasinski, Emily Blunt,

00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:40.498
and Alison Brie train for
physically demanding roles.

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:44.044
- [Jason] My goal for Brie
was to get her resilient,

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:47.464
super strong, so that she
can actually do the stunts

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:50.550
and walk away and not get injured.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.303
- [Narrator] So how did Walsh
get Larson in such good shape

00:00:53.303 --> 00:00:54.763
for this role?

00:00:59.976 --> 00:01:01.853
It wasn't easy.

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:04.481
Larson trained with Walsh
for about nine months

00:01:04.481 --> 00:01:07.275
before "Captain Marvel" started shooting.

00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:10.028
She started her career
as an indie darling,

00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:11.905
gaining critical acclaim for movies like

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:15.158
"Short Term 12" and then
eventually won an Oscar

00:01:15.158 --> 00:01:17.368
for her performance in "Room."

00:01:17.368 --> 00:01:20.789
Playing a superhero is unlike
anything she's done before.

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:22.499
- [Jason] It was a highly
physical role obviously,

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:23.333
she's a superhero,

00:01:23.792 --> 00:01:26.252
so what you want to take
into consideration is

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.421
the day in, day out physical aspects,

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.924
the stunt work that's gonna take place.

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:33.885
She wanted to do most of the stunts.

00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:35.929
- [Narrator] Larson's Instagram
videos of her workouts

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:37.263
have gone viral,

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:39.224
but getting to this point was a battle.

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:41.518
- [Jason] So upper body
strength is very, very important

00:01:41.518 --> 00:01:44.687
for Brie. She came in, she
couldn't perform one push-up,

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:47.565
so we had to start from the
very, very beginning on this.

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:49.192
People see the highlights and think that

00:01:49.192 --> 00:01:51.861
that's just something that
we kinda just jumped into.

00:01:51.861 --> 00:01:54.239
This is something that takes time.

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:58.493
I had three cycles with
her at three-month periods

00:01:58.493 --> 00:02:01.871
where I was able to
progress her each time,

00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:04.249
and it took nine months
to get her to a point

00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:07.210
where she's doing chain
push-ups and things like that.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.171
- [Narrator] Walsh and Alyssa
Shoji, another trainer,

00:02:10.171 --> 00:02:11.923
demonstrated how some of Larson's

00:02:11.923 --> 00:02:13.883
workouts progressed over time.

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:14.717
- [Jason] One of the staples

00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:17.178
that we use here at Rise
Movement is the hip thrust,

00:02:17.178 --> 00:02:19.722
and we do use it for a lot for warm-ups,

00:02:19.722 --> 00:02:22.100
and sometimes we'll use
it, like in Brie's case,

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.935
we use it as a staple exercise

00:02:23.935 --> 00:02:26.020
where you get up to 400
pounds on the hip thrusts,

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.815
but like anything, you need
to start from the bare basics,

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:29.691
learn the movement.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.366
Nice and slow on the tempos,
none of this needs to be fast.

00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:39.659
The next progression we like to do

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:41.494
is to get a bar across the waist here,

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.538
and this is great because
we can track our progress

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:44.664
with the actual weight.

00:02:59.429 --> 00:03:00.638
In order to get to the point where

00:03:00.638 --> 00:03:02.432
she can start from the
top and go to the bottom

00:03:02.432 --> 00:03:06.477
and do the push-up correctly,
we worked on progressions.

00:03:06.477 --> 00:03:10.231
I want the hands spread
about 5 degrees out,

00:03:10.231 --> 00:03:12.483
and I want the elbows to
come back about 45 degrees,

00:03:12.483 --> 00:03:15.486
keep the hips up and engaged here.

00:03:15.486 --> 00:03:18.031
- [Narrator] Once Larson
could do perfect push-ups...

00:03:18.907 --> 00:03:20.533
- [Jason] One of the
highlights we got to with Brie is

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.870
she got strong enough over
a certain amount of time

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.288
that we could do chain push-ups,

00:03:25.288 --> 00:03:27.248
and this is about 50
pounds worth of chain.

00:03:27.248 --> 00:03:29.792
As she lowers all the way to
the ground, it gets lighter.

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:33.004
As she pushes up dynamically,
it gets a little bit heavier.

00:03:33.004 --> 00:03:34.422
So what I would do is drape

00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:39.427
this over the weakest area or
the midpoint over the hips,

00:03:39.636 --> 00:03:42.805
which makes her have to
engage those hips even more,

00:03:42.805 --> 00:03:44.140
core right here.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.892
Now, go ahead.

00:03:47.644 --> 00:03:50.480
It gets lighter. It gets
heavier on the way up.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:51.898
- [Narrator] Then you'd finish with

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:55.693
a few more regular push-ups
under fatigue condition.

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:59.656
Walsh also encourages all sorts
of recreational activities.

00:04:00.615 --> 00:04:03.660
He usually tailors workouts
to the actor's role.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.246
For instance, he made sure Emily Blunt

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:09.707
was able to handle wearing
and moving around in

00:04:09.707 --> 00:04:13.795
a 70-pound exoskeleton
in "Edge of Tomorrow."

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:16.547
He made sure Bradley Cooper
was ready to carry around

00:04:16.547 --> 00:04:19.342
heavy gear while shooting
in desert conditions

00:04:19.342 --> 00:04:21.386
for "American Sniper."

00:04:21.386 --> 00:04:24.305
When he trained with Alison
Brie prior to "GLOW,"

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:26.266
he added exercises to her regimen

00:04:26.266 --> 00:04:29.560
that would prepare her for
the show's wrestling scenes.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.730
Walsh's job wasn't to train
Larson for the actual stunts

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.857
and fights you see her do in the movie.

00:04:34.857 --> 00:04:38.903
His job was to prepare her so
she could then train for that.

00:04:38.903 --> 00:04:40.571
All the little workouts she did

00:04:40.571 --> 00:04:44.200
would perfectly lend themselves
for hand-to-hand combat.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.536
- [Jason] I want the
body to work as a unit.

00:04:46.536 --> 00:04:48.830
Everything that we're doing
is, generally speaking,

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:52.959
full-body workouts, multi-planar
multi-joint exercises.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:56.379
You're able to learn how to
connect the foot and the hip

00:04:56.379 --> 00:04:58.548
and the arms, you're going
to be able to throw a punch

00:04:58.548 --> 00:05:00.800
a lot better than you are
if you're just sitting there

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.344
doing a bunch of bench
press or bicep curls.

00:05:03.344 --> 00:05:04.637
- [Narrator] Her fight training was a mix

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:08.266
of tae kwon do, judo, and boxing.

00:05:08.266 --> 00:05:12.353
Over time, Larson saw
some surprising changes.

00:05:12.353 --> 00:05:15.148
- [Jason] It's pretty funny
actually, the costume designer

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:16.733
was becoming very annoyed with us

00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:19.569
because as we were progressing,
she was getting stronger,

00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:21.904
she was actually getting smaller

00:05:21.904 --> 00:05:24.490
because she was becoming
denser and denser and denser,

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.576
so they actually had to take this suit

00:05:26.576 --> 00:05:28.119
and keep taking it in.

00:05:28.119 --> 00:05:30.455
From the "Avengers" to
the point when we started

00:05:30.455 --> 00:05:32.915
shooting "Captain Marvel," she
was probably half her size,

00:05:32.915 --> 00:05:36.169
but she was 10 times stronger
than she was when she started.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:37.545
- [Narrator] As we mentioned earlier,

00:05:37.545 --> 00:05:40.882
she was even able to
push a Jeep by the end.

00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:44.093
- [Jason] She saw a video of
me doing a stupid exercise

00:05:44.093 --> 00:05:45.178
where I was pulling my Jeep,

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:46.679
and then of course she got it in her mind

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:48.514
that she wanted to be
able to push the Jeep.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:52.143
I told her if I see a
certain level of progression

00:05:52.143 --> 00:05:54.187
and strength with her
and I felt confident,

00:05:54.187 --> 00:05:55.646
then I was like, "Absolutely."

00:05:55.646 --> 00:05:57.607
She was hip thrusting 400 pounds.

00:05:57.607 --> 00:06:00.485
She was doing everything,
surpassed everything

00:06:00.485 --> 00:06:03.321
that I had really anticipated.
She was just hungry for it.

00:06:03.321 --> 00:06:04.989
She loved the challenges.

00:06:04.989 --> 00:06:07.617
She insisted, and I felt it
was a safe enough environment.

00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:08.785
We controlled the environment,

00:06:08.785 --> 00:06:12.580
and she pushed this 5,000-pound
Jeep up a slight grade

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:15.416
in the back alley of my studio.

00:06:15.416 --> 00:06:19.837
The most rewarding thing
for me is to see somebody

00:06:19.837 --> 00:06:23.925
really start to adhere
and absorb and appreciate

00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:26.260
what strength training can do for you.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:29.722
Being pain-free, being
strong, doing things

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:33.976
that you really just didn't
think was possible for you,

00:06:33.976 --> 00:06:34.811
Brie was one of those.

00:06:34.811 --> 00:06:36.270
I think she's a lifer.

